## INTRODUCTION
Immobilization of catalysts in single crystalline microporous materials has been a long-lasting interest in the catalytic community.1,2 In a typical structure, the microporous shells are capable of protecting the fragile catalysts from poisoning by external environment, and the tunable pore size of microporous shells contributes to molecular-size selectivity during reaction. Zeolites, a classic group of microporous materials, have been investigated by many to illustrate this concept.3-5 For example, Christensen and co-workers demonstrated that isolation of Au NPs in silicalite-1 can not only prevent them from sintering under high temperature but also endow this composite with size selective oxidation capability.5 However, the long diffusion pathway in microporous materials inevitably limits the diffusion kinetics of the reactants, which renders the catalytic reaction less efficient.6,7 Moreover, having pore walls in close proximity to the catalyst surface may also hinder its active sites resulting in lower catalytic activity.
One feasible way to circumvent this drawback is to place the catalytic particle amid a single crystalline hollow structure.8,9 The thin wall of the hollow shell effectively shortens the diffusion length therefore ensuring rapid diffusion for reactants while maintaining its inherent molecular sieving and protective functions. More importantly, the large cavity assures full preservation of the native active sites on the catalyst surfaces thereby maximizing its catalytic performance. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a promising class of porous crystalline materials as catalyst support materials.10-17 The wide selection of molecular building blocks for constructing MOFs allow precise manipulation of pore size, pore environment and surface functionality of MOFs to meet specific demands for each catalytic system. However, catalysts encased in single crystalline hollow microporous MOFs are very rare. Although templates such as polystyrene and metal oxide have been used for synthesizing hollow MOFs,18-20 the products are usually polycrystalline consequently bringing grain boundary and defects as new concerns. To the best of our knowledge, ZIF-8 is the only MOF that has been successfully fabricated into hollow single crystalline capsules for hosting metal NPs using an acid etching process.21-23 This process relies on selective breakage of metal-ligand coordination bonds which is difficult to control and generalize. What’s more, the prerequisite to this process is to grow an acid stable MOF shell over an acid labile MOF core, which is already a nontrivial challenge.24-26
Ligand cleavage, on the other hand, is a more promising alternative and may uncover new opportunities. The immense toolbox of organic chemistry offers options to cleave ligands through a range of different mechanisms including redox, radical, and metathesis etc. with high specificity. Recently, Maspoch and co-workers have adopted this methodology to selectively remove $4,4^{\prime}$ - stilbene dicarboxylic acid, a $C=C$ bond containing ligand, through ozonolysis. This process has led to an increase of mesoporosity in a microporous framework without affecting overall MOF crystallinity.27 Moreover, Jiang and co-workers were able to scissor $4,4^{\prime}$ -dipyridyl disulfide, a chemically labile dipyridyl ligand, through disulfide reduction. This has led to MOF exfoliation affording ultrathin 2D MOF nanosheets.28 However, this method has not been demonstrated for the fabrication of hierarchical MOF materials with complex architectures.
Here we report a new strategy, namely oxidative linker cleaving (OLC), to precisely manipulate the engraving process within core-shell Zr MOFs.29 This approach uses 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalicacid (DOBDC) as a sacrificial linker which can be selectively cleaved into small molecular fragments via oxidative ring-opening reactions. By controlling the generation of oxidative species, UiO$66\mathrm{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ can be directionally engraved either from inside to outside or outside to inside, leading to a series of single crystalline hollow and yolk-shell UiO-66 structures (Scheme 1). Moreover, by pre-imbedding redox active palladium (Pd) NPs in a MOF particle, the OLC process can be initiated and localized around the Pd NPs resulting in solitary confinement of multiple Pd NPs within a single crystalline MOF particle, providing a so-called multi-yolkshell (MYS) structure.
