## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
We first fabricated a core-shell MOF, UiO-66-(OH)2@UiO66-Br, to demonstrate the selective etching process. Ideally, the DOBDC containing core can be oxidatively cleaved whereas the shell is resistant to oxidation. It began with the synthesis of uniform UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ crystals with average size of ${\sim}400\ \mathsf{n m}$ according to a reported method (Figure S1). Then the UiO-66-Br shell was epitaxially grown on UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ core. The select area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern confirmed the single crystallinity of the core-shell structure (Figure 1A). ${\mathsf{H N O}}_{3},$ a common acid oxidant for bond cleavage, was used to demonstrate this concept.30 After treating $\mathsf{U i O-66-(O H)_{2}@U i O-66-B r}$ with $H N O_{3}$ for $5\mathsf{h}$ , the center of the core-shell particle started collapsing leaving a ${\sim}200~\mathsf{n m}$ cavity as seen under TEM. This cavity then gradually propagated outwards eventually leading to a well-defined hollow UiO-66-Br structure with a $400\ \mathsf{n m}$ cavity (Figure 1(Bi-Biii)). The size of the cavity matched well with the size of $\mathsf{U i O}\mathsf{-}66\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ particles suggesting complete removal of the UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ core. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of the hollow UiO-66-Br manifests perfect octahedral-shaped particles with no sign of deformation or surface pinhole formation (Figure 1Biii and Figure S2). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern and the SAED pattern of hollow UiO-66-Br particle reveal that hollow UiO-66-Br
shell remains crystalline after $H N O_{3}$ etching (Figure 1D; Figure 1Biii). ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $77~\mathsf{K}$ show that the micropore uptake $(\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}<0.1)$ of UiO-66- $\cdot(\mathsf{O H})_{2}@\mathsf{U i O-}$ 66-Br first decreased then gradually increased with extended etching time (Figure S3). The final hollow UiO66-Br exhibits no loss of microporosity or surface area compared to that of solid UiO-66-Br particles (Figure 1E). In addition, mesoporosity became more prominent as suggested by the pore size distribution data (Figure S4). This is likely due to the locate dissolution and defect generation within the shell layer after harsh treatment.
In contrast to ${\mathsf{H N O}}_{3},$ reactive oxygen species (ROS), a group of oxygen-containing radicals (e.g. $50_{4}^{\cdots};2.5{-}3.1\vee$ vs NHE; •OH: 1.9–2.7 V) are much stronger oxidizers.31 They can be generated through catalytic activation of peroxymonosulfate (PMS) under acidic, neutral or alkaline conditions by various metal ions,32 metal ${\mathsf{N P S}},{}^{33}$ alloy ${\mathsf{N P S}}^{34}$ and metallic oxides35 (Figure S5). We hypothesize that the strong oxidizing capacity and short half-lives of ROS $^{\cdot}{<}1$ µs for •OH and $30{-}40~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ for $\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{\bullet-}$ ) would allow us to precisely control the OLC process.36 Here, ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ was selected as a catalyst for ROS generation. Due to the low concentration of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ catalyst $(0.005~\mathsf{M})$ , the diffusion of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ catalysts through UiO-66-Br shell is negligible (Figure S6). Therefore, the ROS products are mainly generated outside the MOF. As expected, after treating UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}@\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-B r}$ with a ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{M}}{\mathsf{S}}$ solution mixture, ROS produced by ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{M}}{\mathsf{S}}$ system resulted in inward engraving of the UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ core, as evidenced by the continuous evolution from yolk-shell to hollow structures (Figure 1(Ci-Ciii)). The hollow UiO-66-Br obtained via ROS etching also exhibits completely retention of microporosity and crystallinity as confirmed by its PXRD pattern, SAED pattern and ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption experiment (Figure 1D, Ciii, and E). Benefiting from the relatively mild etching condition of the ROS process, the pre-imbedded Pd NPs within $\mathsf{U i O-66-(O H)_{2}@U i O-66-B r}$ remained intact after etching affording a yolk-shell structure containing multiple Pd NPs within a single crystalline hollow UiO-66- Br shell (Figure S9). For simplification, this structure is denoted as YS-Pd⊂UiO-66-Br. To demonstrate the generalizability of these etching processes, a series of single crystalline hollow UiO-66 with different functional groups $(-H,-C H_{3},-N H_{2},-N O_{2},$ -NDC) were prepared either using $H N O_{3}$ or ROS (Figure S7-S11).
To understand the OLC mechanism triggered by ROS and $\mathsf{H N O}_{3},$ the decomposed products of UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ were analyzed by $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR and LC-MS. Small molecular fragments such as formic acid and oxalic acid were identified to be the main products after ROS and $H N O_{3}$ etching processes, respectively (detailed descriptions are shown in Section S3). These results suggest that, in both cases, the C-C bonds in the DOBDC linker are the scissoring point of the $\mathsf{U i O}\mathsf{-}66\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ core. To further elucidate the reasoning behind etching directionality, we monitored the residual DOBDC ligand at various time points using $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR. During ROS etching, the decrease of DOBDC coinciding with the visual disappearance of the UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ core (Figure 1F). This suggests that due to the short lifetime and high reactivity of ${\mathsf{R O S}},$ it reacted rapidly with DOBDC and was quickly scavenged before diffusing into the core. Therefore, the etching process occurred in a layer-by-layer fashion, just as illustrated in Figure S12. $H N O_{3}$ etching, however, occurs much slower due to its lower oxidation potential compared to ROS. $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ crystals underwent at least two-step chemical reaction during $H N O_{3}$ treatment. First, DOBDC was oxidized to 2,5-dihydroxy-3-nitroterephthalic acid (DONTA) as $H N O_{3}$ diffused inwards. The presence of DONTA as intermediate is evidenced by FT-IR, $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR as well as LC-MS (Figure S13-S15). At this stage, the microporous framework structure of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ remained largely intact as evidenced by the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm (Figure S16). The slightly reduced uptake capacity compared to that of UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ is likely due to the addition of nitro group in the framework that occupies extra pore volume. Despite the disappearance of DOBDC, the TEM images also show that no etching or particle collapse until $5h\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ treatment (the insets in Figure 1F). After ${\mathsf{I}}{\mathsf{h}},$ the etching process started from the center of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ crystals through the OLC process (Figure 1Bi). This is likely because the center of a MOF particle often contains more defects thus prone to etching.37,38 During the collapse process, the outer layer still maintains its crystal structure (Figure S17). These results clearly suggest that OLC triggered directional etching process can be precisely regulated by controlling the diffusion of oxidants.
Concluding from the previous mechanistic study, we demonstrated that ROS triggered etching process is highly controllable, and the etching directionality can be customized by manipulating the placement of the catalyst. By replacing ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ion catalyst with pre-imbedded Pd NPs within UiO-66- $(O H)_{2},$ the etching can, in principle, be initiated and localized around Pd NPs (Figure 2C). For proof-of-concept, PVP coated Pd NPs with an average size of ${\sim}25~\mathsf{n m}$ was introduced during the synthesis of UiO$66\mathrm{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ which resulted in the inclusion of multiple Pd NPs randomly distributed in a single crystalline UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ particle $(\mathsf{P d}\mathsf{c}\mathsf{U i}0\mathsf{-}66\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2})$ . After incubation with PMS aqueous solution for $1.5\mathsf{h},$ isolated cavities appeared around each Pd NPs leading to a so-called multi-yolkshell (MYS) structure. For simplification, this structure is denoted as MYS-Pd⊂UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ . As obtained MYS$P d{\mathsf{\subset U i O}}{\mathsf{-66{\bar{-}}(O H)}}_{2}$ remains crystalline and porous as evidenced by its PXRD pattern and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm (Figure S18 and S19). The SEM images also suggest that no apparent morphological changes can be observed before and after etching (Figure S20). Furthermore, by introducing a ROS scavenger, methanol,39 at different concentrations during the etching process, the etching kinetics can be regulated resulting in continuous tuning of cavity sizes (Figure S21).
To demonstrate the size selective catalytic properties of $\mathsf{M Y S–P d C U i O–66–(O H)_{2}},$ liquid-phase catalytic hydrogenation of cyclohexene and cyclooctene were carried out. The kinetic diameter of cyclohexene $(4.2\mathbb{A})^{19}$ is comparable to the aperture size of $\mathsf{U i O}\mathsf{-}66\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ $\mathord{\leftrightharpoons}4$ $\mathbb{A})^{40}$ whereas cyclooctene features a considerably larger kinetic diameter $(5.5\mathrm{\textrm{\AA}})^{19}$ and should in principle be discriminated by the UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ shell. Under identical catalytic condition, $97\%$ cyclohexene was hydrogenated by $M Y S\mathrm{-}P d C\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathrm{-}(O H)_{2}$ . In sharp contrast, no cyclooctene hydrogenation was observed which strongly manifested the sharp size cut-off effect of the single crystalline $\mathsf{U i O}\mathsf{-}66\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ shell (Figure 3A). Meanwhile, when MYS-Pd⊂UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ was compared with $P d{\mathsf{\subset U i O}}{\mathsf{-66{\bar{-}}(O H)}}_{2}$ on 1-octylene and allylbenzene hydrogenation, the MYS structure shows $80\%$ conversion for both 1-octylene and allylbenzene, considerably higher than that of $P d{\mathsf{\subset U i O}}{\mathsf{-66{\bar{-}}(O H)}}_{2}$ $56.5\%$ and $50.7\%,$ respectively) (Figure 3B). This conspicuous difference still remained even after the reaction time was prolonged to near completion (Figure S22). This is a clear evidence that constructing MYS architecture is an effective way to increase the diffusion kinetics during catalytic reactions without compromising its molecular sieving property. Furthermore, the unique solitarily confined architecture is ideal for preventing the pernicious thermal sintering of metal NPs. After purposefully heating MYS-Pd⊂UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ and YS-Pd⊂UiO-66-Br at $200~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in ${\sf N}_{2}$ for $12\ h,$ severe NP agglomeration was only observed in YSPd⊂UiO-66-Br (Figure 3F and 3G). This has led to a drastic drop of its allylbenzene hydrogenation activity from an initial $80.3\%$ conversion to merely $23.8\%$ (Figure 3C). On the contrary, $\mathsf{M Y S–P d C U i O–66–(O H)_{2}}$ retained its full hydrogenation reactivity for at least 5 consecutive cycles after identical thermal treatment (Figure S23 and S24).
## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, we developed an OLC process to accurately manipulate the engraving process within single crystalline core-shell Zr-MOFs. This process relies on selective oxidative cleavage of DOBDC linker into small molecular fragments, leading to the controllable collapse of UiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ core. The different oxidation capability of $H N O_{3}$ and ROS led to opposed engraving directionality yet similar hollow MOF end products. Furthermore, pre-imbedded Pd NPs within the MOF allows on-site generation of ROS around Pd resulting in a so-called MYS structure that is a promising architecture for preventing metal NP thermal sintering. We believe the controllable OLC processes demonstrated in this work provides new inspirations for designing sophisticated MOF/metal NP composite catalysts.
